content.thinking:
Concentrate.prototype.result returns a Buffer. Replace encode with `.toString('base64url')` and decode with `Buffer.from(treeHash, 'base64url')`.

Note: urlsafe-base64's decode may strip padding etc. Node's base64url accepts both padded/unpadded. Also, does urlsafe-base64.encode produce padding? urlsafe-base64 encode: it replaces +/ and removes padding ('=')? Checking: urlsafe-base64 encode does `.replace(/\//g, '_').replace(/\+/g, '-').replace(/=+$/, '')`? Actually urlsafe-base64 keeps... From memory, the `urlsafe-base64` package strips padding? Let me think: urlsafe-base64's encode: `base64.toString('base64').replace(/\+/g, '-').replace(/\//g, '_')` — I believe it does NOT strip padding by default? Actually looking at the package source: 

```js
exports.encode = function base64urlEncode(buffer) {
  return buffer.toString('base64')
    .replace(/=/g, '')
    ...
```

Hmm, I recall urlsafe-base64 does remove padding in encode: from its readme "URL Safe Base64 util for Node.js" — encode strips '=' padding. Actually the source:

```js
'use strict';
function fromBase64(base64) { return base64.replace(/=/g,'').replace(/\+/g,'-').replace(/\//g,'_'); }
function toBase64(base64) {
  base64 = base64.toString().replace(/\-/g,'+').replace(/_/g,'/');
  while (base64.length % 4) base64 += '=';
  return base64;
}
exports.encode = function (buf) { return fromBase64(buf.toString('base64')); };
exports.decode = function (base64) { return Buffer.from(toBase64(base64), 'base64'); };
```

Node's `toString('base64url')` produces unpadded output too (base64url in Node omits padding? Actually Node's base64url encoding omits padding characters). Yes — Node base64url doesn't include padding. And `Buffer.from(str, 'base64url')` accepts unpadded input. So 1:1 compatible.

Also important: persisted hashes in manifests from previous versions are unpadded — new code must decode them, which works.

